From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:15:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: show the old WhatsApp messages the phone sends
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

* Show the old WhatsApp messages the phone sends

The phone sends a newly linked device its recent messages (a
HistorySync of type INITIAL_BOOTSTRAP, then RECENT and perhaps FULL),
and sends more whenever the device asks for the messages before one it
has (ON_DEMAND). Only the names in it were read, so a newly linked
device showed no chat until someone wrote, and nothing older than that.

Each old message now goes the way a live one does, with its own id,
time, sender and direction, so the transport keeps it under its id and
stores one it has already only once (its serviceMessageId dedup). It
is marked as history: the glue gives it even with "discard old
messages" on, and sets "webos-msg-history" on its conversation for
just as long as libpurple hands it on, which the transport takes to
store it as read and without a banner. Its picture, video, voice
note, sticker or document is named in words ("📷 Photo" and the
caption, "📄 Q3.pdf"), not downloaded: a sync can name thousands, the
phone keeps old media only so long, and the download would hold up the
main loop. A vote and a shared history are left out, and old messages
stay out of the message cache, which holds the recent ones a reply or
reaction looks up, and wait for no read receipt.

How much: from a sync the phone sends by itself, each chat's newest
messages up to the new account option "history-messages-per-chat"
(default 50, 0 for none), none older than 90 days. Channels and status
updates are left alone. What the device asked for comes as asked, up
to 200 of a chat.

The device asks when the transport emits "webos-im-load-history"
(account, chat, the id of the oldest message it has, that message's
time in seconds or milliseconds, "1" when we sent it): the plugin
sends the phone a history request for the 50 messages before it,
under the chat's phone number and, when the store knows it, its LID,
as which of the two the phone keeps the chat under is not known here.

glue/tests/display_message_test checks that the mark is there while
the transport takes the message, received or ours, in a chat or a
group, and gone at once after. history_sync_test checks which
messages are given, how many, and how media is named.

* Say when WhatsApp disappearing messages change, and what a view-once message is

Turning disappearing messages on or off, or changing their timer, said
nothing. In a chat it comes as a ProtocolMessage of type
EPHEMERAL_SETTING with no text, which handle_message dropped; in a
group as a group change (events.GroupInfo.Ephemeral), which
group_event_notices did not tell. Both are now a notice line with the
time of the change, in the WhatsApp app's words: "Ann turned on
disappearing messages. New messages will disappear from this chat 7
days after they're sent.", "You turned off disappearing messages."
The messages themselves are kept here as any other: this device does
not delete them when their time is up.

A view-once photo, video or voice message is not given to a linked
device. It gets an "unavailable" message of type view_once instead,
which read "sent an undecryptable view_once message. Check the message
on your main device.", under the chat's LID when WhatsApp addressed it
so, which is not the conversation the contact's messages are in. It now reads "View once
message. Open it on your phone.", in the chat as the conversation
knows it, by phone number. Should a view-once message come with its
media all the same (an older sender's ViewOnceMessage), the media is
neither downloaded nor kept: the message says "📷 View once photo.
Open it on your phone." (video, voice message).

disappearing_test checks the words, which messages set the timer (a
revoke, protocol type 0, does not), a group's change on and off, and
which messages count as view-once.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
